Subject: Informed Consent, not Non-coercion

ISIL's prez and veep once played me a tape of a talk given by Leon Llouw
on libertarian jurisprudence in which he advocated that we substitute the
term "informed consent" for "non-coercion" or "non-aggression."

(For those that may not know: he and his mate, Frances Kendall, have been
nominated more than once for the Nobel Peace Prize for their advocacy of
a decentralist solution to South Africa's constitutional problems along
Swiss lines.  He's a lawyer who's also been commissioned to develop
constitutions for former USSR client-states in Siberia like Irkutsk, if
my memory serves me correctly.  They wrote: "After Apartheid: The Solution
for South Africa," which is one of the best-selling books in South African
history, founded Groundswell and the Free Market Foundation.)

I tend to agree.  It has the advantage of greater currency in jurisprudence,
as well as being a positive rather than a negative.

