Date: 27 Mar 93 14:09:00 EST
From: "Thomas D Burns" <TBURNS@gmuvax.gmu.edu>
Subject: Subject: PHIL: Are we hypocrites?

X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

>Garrett says: 
>> o  What defines AC/libertarianism/etc... what are the root principles?
>
Dave Burns says:
AC has to do with means, libertarianism with ends. AC has
to do with what institutions/rules/culture (mostly property rights) coordinates
persons. Set up a self-correcting process & see where it goes. Libertarianism
is a guess about where society would go; very few rigid restrictions on
behavior. 

Perry says:
The only decent way to define a libertarian is as a
>person who believes in the non-coercion principle, which can be
>defined many ways. [. . .]

Garrett syas:
>> o  Exactly what kinds of force are allowed vs. what is voluntary?

Dave says:
I'm beginning to think that almost everyone believes a variant of
the n-cp. The difference between a statist and a libertarian has to
do with how they define harm, violence, initiation of force, voluntariness.
A statist would claim that these are captured appropriately by the political
process as it is. A libertarian would argue for different criteria for what
constitutes initiation of force. One path to libertarian reform of the
political process is Anarcho-Capitalism.

Garrett:
>> o  Do basic rights exist?  Is there a point where I should be able to
>>   force my principles on another? ...where my or their principles are
>>   just fundamentally right and merit initiation of force.
>
Dave:
The move people usually make is to just redefine whatever you don't like
to be initiation of force. I think it is quite rare that people self-
consciously initiate force. The BATF thinks the Davidians initiated
force, and the Davidians think the opposite. I wouldn't be surprised
if most theives and murderer justified their actions by some perceived 
injustice they're willing to blame on their victim. 

Perry:
>These questions are all ill posed. Asking if basic rights exist is
>an abuse of language. What does "exist" mean? [ . . .]
Dave:
Isn't that a bit strong? I agree that talking about the existence of
rights is beginning in the wrong place, but that is quite common usage
of language, and typically is where people do begin. The real problem
is that that question is really about 7 questions: What rules should
be enforced, what are the consequences of breaking them, are they in 
some sense enforced by the universe like laws of physics, who may 
enforce them, etc.
The main question: When should I feel entitled to do something to
someone or someone's property that that person would not agree to?
Law is the process of answering that question. 
